module

-- **OPEN.** This file is yours: it contains work you have to do, here, and
-- nothing will fill it in. A signature install leaves it alone.
--
-- **The seam every generated rule proof imports.** The compiler emits each rule
-- as a statement in terms of the names below, so this is the one module that
-- has to supply them before any rule can be built.
--
-- It is a stub, and it is the reason `scripts/build-rules.sh` reports that no
-- rule can be built yet. Filling it in is the start of the correctness
-- development, not a detail of it.
--
-- What a rule statement refers to, and so what this must export:
--
--   `model_wf M`               that `M` is a well-formed SMT model
--   `premiseTermList s ps`     the terms a step's premises name in state `s`
--   `AllHaveBoolType ts`       that every one of them is Boolean-typed
--   `StepRuleProperties M ts`  what a rule must establish about its conclusion,
--                              given what its premises say under `M` -- the
--                              contract the checker's core relies on
--   `cmdTranslationOk c`       from `Proofs/Assumptions.lean`, re-exported here
--
-- To see the exact shape the compiler expects, read any file under
-- `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/`: each is a statement about these names, with
-- `sorry` where its proof goes.
--
-- In Logos this is `Cpc/Proofs/RuleSupport/Support.lean`, a thin aggregator over
-- `Proofs/Common.lean`, `Proofs/RuleSupport/Contract.lean` and the per-theory
-- support libraries beneath them -- some 350,000 lines. Do not expect to write
-- it in one sitting; `StepRuleProperties` and the model well-formedness
-- predicate are the load-bearing definitions, and the rest accretes as rules
-- are proven.
--
-- TEMPLATE STUB: scripts/build-rules.sh looks for this marker to explain why
-- rule proofs cannot be built. Remove the line when this module is real.
public import @CALCULUS@.Proofs.CheckerCore
import all @CALCULUS@.Proofs.CheckerCore

@[expose] public section

namespace Eo

end Eo
